---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.5 — Loop Analysis, Fencepost Traps &amp; The LoopTracer Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.1, 2.9 · 90-Minute Unified Build.</strong> On the AP CS A exam, nearly a
    quarter of Multiple Choice questions ask you to determine how many times a loop executes, spot
    an off-by-one ("fencepost") error, or trace a loop whose variable both gains and loses value
    within the cycle. Today you build <code>LoopTracer.java</code> and confront the three nastiest
    loop-analysis traps on the exam.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Starting Files (Download Both):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/LoopTracer.java" download>LoopTracer.java</a> — bare-minimum compiling
            stub.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/LoopTracerCheck.java" download>LoopTracerCheck.java</a> — automated
            AP-style test suite (17 checks across 4 parts).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>javac LoopTracer.java LoopTracerCheck.java
java LoopTracerCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Parts 1 &amp; 2 · Fenceposts &amp; Iteration Counts</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~40 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>countWhileIterations(int start, int limit, int step)</code></h3>
    <p>
        Return the exact number of times the body of this loop executes:
    </p>
    <pre><code>int val = start;
while (val &lt; limit) {
    // body
    val += step;
}</code></pre>
    <ul>
        <li>If <code>start &gt;= limit</code>, the condition is immediately <code>false</code> on
            the first check, so the loop runs <code>0</code> times (even if <code>step &lt;= 0</code>!).</li>
        <li>If <code>start &lt; limit</code> and <code>step &lt;= 0</code>, the loop would run
            forever; return <code>-1</code> without hanging.</li>
        <li>Otherwise, simulate or compute the exact number of iterations.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>buildRunedFence(String rune, int pillars)</code></h3>
    <p>
        Build a fence banner containing <code>pillars</code> copies of <code>"[" + rune + "]"</code>
        connected by <code>"---"</code> dividers:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>buildRunedFence("*", 3)</code> returns <code>"[*]---[*]---[*]"</code> (3 pillars,
            <strong>2</strong> dividers).</li>
        <li><code>buildRunedFence("SOL", 1)</code> returns <code>"[SOL]"</code> (1 pillar,
            <strong>0</strong> dividers).</li>
        <li>If <code>pillars &lt;= 0</code>, return <code>""</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 1: The Fencepost Problem</h3>
        <p>
            To build a 3-pillar fence, there are <code>3</code> pillars (<code>[*]</code>) but only
            <code>2</code> spans of wire (<code>---</code>) between them.
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>If a loop simply runs <code>pillars</code> times and does
                <code>result += "[" + rune + "]---";</code> every iteration, what does
                <code>buildRunedFence("*", 3)</code> return, and why does it fail?</li>
            <li>Explain the "Fencepost Pattern": how do you place the first post outside the loop
                (or check the loop index) so there are always <code>pillars - 1</code> dividers for
                <code>pillars</code> posts?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Parts 3 &amp; 4 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~50 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>firstTurnReachingMana(int startMana, int gainPerTurn, int drainEveryThirdTurn, int targetMana)</code></h3>

    <p>
        A mage starts with <code>startMana</code>. If <code>startMana &gt;= targetMana</code>
        before Turn 1 even begins, return <code>0</code>. Otherwise, during each turn
        <code>turn = 1, 2, 3, ...</code>:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>The mage gains <code>gainPerTurn</code> mana.</li>
        <li>If <code>turn</code> is a multiple of <code>3</code> (<code>turn % 3 == 0</code>), a
            curse drains <code>drainEveryThirdTurn</code> mana during that same turn.</li>
        <li>At the end of the turn (after any Turn-3 drain), check if mana is now
            <code>&gt;= targetMana</code>. If so, return <code>turn</code>!</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        If the mage will <strong>never</strong> reach <code>targetMana</code>, return
        <code>-1</code> without an infinite loop.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 2: The Peak-Before-Drain Trap</h3>
        <p>
            Over every 3-turn cycle, the mage's net mana change is
            <code>3 * gainPerTurn - drainEveryThirdTurn</code>. Suppose a programmer writes this
            infinite-loop check at the top of the method:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Naive guard — why does this fail Check 4 in Part 3?
if (3 * gainPerTurn - drainEveryThirdTurn &lt;= 0) {
    return -1;
}</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>Trace <code>firstTurnReachingMana(6, 2, 10, 10)</code>: the mage starts at
                <code>6</code> mana, gains <code>2</code> per turn, loses <code>10</code> on Turn
                3, and needs <code>10</code> mana. Even though the 3-turn net change is negative
                (<code>6 - 10 = -4</code>), what is the mage's mana at the end of Turn 1 and Turn
                2? Why does the mage win on Turn 2 <em>before</em> the Turn-3 drain ever fires?</li>
            <li>Why is it safe to simulate Turn 1, Turn 2, and Turn 3 first, and only return
                <code>-1</code> if the mage hasn't reached <code>targetMana</code> by the end of
                Turn 3 AND their mana at the end of Turn 3 is <code>&lt;= startMana</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>countTriangularSteps(int n)</code></h3>
    <p>
        Return the total number of times <code>steps++</code> executes in this nested loop (or
        <code>0</code> if <code>n &lt;= 0</code>):
    </p>
    <pre><code>int steps = 0;
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; i++) {
    for (int j = i; j &lt;= n; j += 2) {
        steps++;
    }
}</code></pre>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><code>java LoopTracerCheck</code> reports <strong><code>17 passed, 0 failed</code></strong></li>
            <li>You have completed the reflection boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/loop-analysis.html">Tracing Loops</a> — trace tables, off-by-one, iteration counts</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
